Question No: 33. Search in Rotated Sorted Array

The Solution is in C++ Language.

Solution:
class Solution {
public:
    int search(vector<int>& nums, int target) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.size() - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int mid = (left + right) / 2;

            if (nums[mid] == target) {
                return mid;
            } else if (nums[mid] >= nums[left]) {
                if (nums[left] <= target && target <= nums[mid]) {
                    right = mid - 1;
                } else {
                    left = mid + 1;
                }
            } else {
                if (nums[mid] <= target && target <= nums[right]) {
                    left = mid + 1;
                } else {
                    right = mid - 1;
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;        
    }
};

Approach:
The approach uses modified binary search because the array is rotated but still has one sorted half. 
We find the middle index and check whether the target is found. 
If the left half is sorted (nums[mid] >= nums[left]), we check whether the target lies between nums[left] and nums[mid]; if yes, we search left, otherwise right. 
If the right half is sorted, we check whether the target lies between nums[mid] and nums[right] and search accordingly. 
We repeat until the target is found or the search range becomes empty. If not found, we return -1.

Time Complexity: O(log n).
Space Complexity: O(1).
